/**
 * Two-layer position-wise MLP with a SiLU activation — the non-gated projection head that
 * embeds a conditioning signal into a model's hidden width. It is the block-level counterpart
 * of the producer LayerFeatures.siluMlp (linear(silu(linear(x)))), written out as data flow.
 *
 * This file replaces the Java assembly that used to live in
 * DiffusionTransformerFeatures.timestepEmbedding(...) (the linear -> SiLU -> linear tail that
 * follows the Fourier feature projection) and DiffusionFeatures.timestepEmbeddings(...), both of
 * which built this same structure as a SequentialBlock (dense, SiLU, dense). Those methods now
 * only bind the two projections and load this asset, so there is one definition of the structure
 * and it is here.
 *
 * The Fourier feature stage that precedes this MLP in a full timestep embedding is NOT part of
 * this asset: the Fourier features are [cos(f), sin(f)] over a projected scalar. concat_blocks is
 * now differentiable (it slices the output gradient back to each part), so this stage could move
 * into PDSL; that move is tracked separately and has not been made yet, so the Fourier projection
 * still stays in Java and feeds this MLP as the layer's input.
 *
 * Data flow of one forward pass over a conditioning vector [..., in]:
 *
 *   [..., in] conditioning vector
 *     dense(w_in, b_in)                                  [..., hidden]
 *     silu()                                             [..., hidden]
 *     dense(w_out, b_out)                                [..., out]
 *
 * SiLU-MLP(x) = w_out . silu(w_in . x + b_in) + b_out, with silu(z) = z * sigmoid(z). w_in is the
 * first projection [hidden, in], w_out the second projection [out, hidden]; both carry a bias.
 *
 * Stage 1 dense(w_in, b_in) projects the input to the hidden width. Stage 2 silu applies the SiLU
 * gate element-wise, preserving the shape. Stage 3 dense(w_out, b_out) projects the activated
 * hidden vector to the output width.
 */
layer silu_mlp(w_in: weight, b_in: weight, w_out: weight, b_out: weight) {
    dense(w_in, b_in)
    silu()
    dense(w_out, b_out)
}
